WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.060
So the question here is, what do you

00:02.060 --> 00:04.380
need to know to really get started with Vue?

00:04.380 --> 00:06.480
And so earlier, we talked about some prerequisites

00:06.480 --> 00:09.260
for the workshop, but this is where I kind of want to start

00:09.260 --> 00:12.420
us as far as ground zero goes.

00:12.420 --> 00:16.100
As long as you have familiarity with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript,

00:16.100 --> 00:18.700
and you have familiarity with the code editor,

00:18.700 --> 00:21.020
that is all you need.

00:21.020 --> 00:22.980
And that might come as a surprise to people,

00:22.980 --> 00:24.940
because a lot of times when we think about frameworks,

00:24.940 --> 00:27.420
we are thinking about it from a build tool perspective.

00:27.420 --> 00:29.420
But we are going to go ahead and show you

00:29.420 --> 00:31.340
how this approach works and why it's actually

00:31.340 --> 00:34.380
a viable approach when it comes to a lot of organizations

00:34.380 --> 00:37.340
looking to either progressively migrate their apps

00:37.340 --> 00:39.700
into a framework.

00:39.700 --> 00:43.540
So here's your Morpheus meme from Matrix.

00:43.540 --> 00:48.580
What if I told you no build tools were needed?

00:48.580 --> 00:51.100
And so again, some of you will be understandably skeptical,

00:51.100 --> 00:53.940
but we're going to actually experience this live ourselves

00:53.940 --> 00:58.060
and go through what this is like.

00:58.060 --> 01:00.380
So with that said, has everyone had a chance

01:00.380 --> 01:02.820
to basically fork the repo, sort of clone it down

01:02.820 --> 01:05.500
to make sure they have that environment ready to go?

01:05.500 --> 01:07.500
OK.

01:07.500 --> 01:09.980
Great.

01:09.980 --> 01:11.900
So the first thing we're going to do,

01:11.900 --> 01:14.900
we talked for a little bit now, let's dive right into some code

01:14.900 --> 01:16.660
and check this out.

01:16.660 --> 01:19.860
So inside of here, you'll notice that there's very little

01:19.860 --> 01:20.540
going on in here.

01:20.540 --> 01:21.900
There's no package.json.

01:21.900 --> 01:23.580
There's no dependencies to run.

01:23.580 --> 01:25.140
It's basically a readme file here,

01:25.140 --> 01:27.820
and we have a couple of HTML files for you

01:27.820 --> 01:29.700
to basically work with.

01:29.700 --> 01:31.580
So what I'm going to do is I'm going

01:31.580 --> 01:34.340
to go ahead and open up my playground HTML

01:34.340 --> 01:37.300
file inside of the browser over here.

01:37.300 --> 01:39.100
And so to make sure everything's working,

01:39.100 --> 01:42.740
I'm just going to go ahead and do an H1 with hello, front end

01:42.740 --> 01:46.340
masters, save, refresh.

01:46.340 --> 01:46.860
There we go.

01:46.860 --> 01:47.740
OK.

01:47.740 --> 01:49.740
Dev environment set up, no build tools required.

01:49.740 --> 01:52.020
Great.

01:52.020 --> 01:56.260
Now, when we think about building your first view app,

01:56.260 --> 01:58.420
I mentioned before, because no build tools are required,

01:58.420 --> 02:02.020
what you can do is you can actually just drop view onto a page.

02:02.020 --> 02:03.340
And so the way we're going to do that

02:03.340 --> 02:08.380
is we're going to go ahead and go onto the view.js.org docs.

02:08.380 --> 02:13.500
And if we go to the guide, I believe it's under quick start,

02:13.500 --> 02:15.740
we'll see.

02:15.740 --> 02:16.900
Here we go.

02:16.900 --> 02:19.500
There's a section here that says using view from the CDN.

02:19.500 --> 02:20.620
And all we're going to do is we're

02:20.620 --> 02:23.900
going to copy this line right here and drop it into our HTML

02:23.900 --> 02:26.580
file.

02:26.580 --> 02:30.140
So all we're going to do here is boom, paste that in.

02:30.140 --> 02:31.900
And so to verify that this worked,

02:31.900 --> 02:33.660
that something actually happened, we're

02:33.660 --> 02:37.020
actually going to go ahead and open up our browser console.

02:37.020 --> 02:39.460
And you'll notice here that, one, it's already alerting us

02:39.460 --> 02:41.860
that we're running a development build of view.

02:41.860 --> 02:43.860
So in other words, if we're going to deploy this,

02:43.860 --> 02:45.860
there's going to need to use a production deployment,

02:45.860 --> 02:48.340
because the size is going to be a bit larger.

02:48.340 --> 02:50.420
But this is fine, because we're learning right now.

02:50.420 --> 02:52.260
So the way you can check that also that this is working

02:52.260 --> 02:54.580
is if you type view, it'll auto-complete.

02:54.580 --> 02:57.380
And you'll notice it'll basically bring up the view object that's

02:57.380 --> 03:00.300
ready for you to do stuff with it.

03:00.300 --> 03:01.740
And so what I'm going to do right now

03:01.740 --> 03:04.940
is I'm going to go ahead and just show you basically

03:04.940 --> 03:06.580
how that kind of works.

03:06.580 --> 03:09.300
So what I'm going to do here is we're going to go ahead

03:09.300 --> 03:10.860
and we're going to create a div.

03:10.860 --> 03:12.460
And just like you typically would expect,

03:12.460 --> 03:15.100
I'm going to call it the div app.

03:15.100 --> 03:17.900
That's all we're going to do.

03:17.900 --> 03:21.140
And then we're going to say, my app in here, save it,

03:21.140 --> 03:21.820
and we refresh.

03:21.820 --> 03:24.020
There you go, my app is showing up here.

03:24.020 --> 03:26.060
And now let's go ahead and actually attach our app.

03:26.060 --> 03:27.060
And how do we do that?

03:27.060 --> 03:28.500
So what we're going to do now is we're

03:28.500 --> 03:30.740
going to open up a script block at the bottom.

03:30.740 --> 03:33.820
And we're actually going to go ahead and bring in a function

03:33.820 --> 03:35.860
from view called create app.

03:35.860 --> 03:37.220
So I'm going to create a constant.

03:37.220 --> 03:40.100
And I'm going to destructure it from view.

03:40.100 --> 03:43.260
So it's going to look like this.

03:43.260 --> 03:46.280
Now, this is an alternative syntax that basically just

03:46.280 --> 03:52.160
calling create app like this, calling the function directly.

03:52.160 --> 03:53.600
But there will be an advantage to this

03:53.600 --> 03:54.800
from a syntax perspective.

03:54.800 --> 03:57.160
So that's why we're destructuring the function out.

03:57.160 --> 03:58.320
And all we're going to do is we're

03:58.320 --> 04:02.200
going to go ahead and say const app equals create app.

04:05.800 --> 04:09.080
And so what that'll do is it'll create a variable called app.

04:09.080 --> 04:11.800
And then it'll go ahead and create set app.

04:11.800 --> 04:14.360
And all we're going to do now is we're going to mount.

04:14.360 --> 04:15.280
Because what we've got to do, we've

04:15.280 --> 04:18.320
got to tell view where it is we want to actually put the app.

04:18.320 --> 04:20.720
So that is where this mount keyword comes in.

04:20.720 --> 04:24.360
And we're going to mount it to the ID of app, which

04:24.360 --> 04:26.000
is using the CSS selector here.

04:26.000 --> 04:29.960
So it'll mount it directly into this part.

04:29.960 --> 04:33.720
So in three lines of codes, we have dropped the view,

04:33.720 --> 04:36.960
or I guess four of you include the CDN.

04:36.960 --> 04:39.000
We've loaded view onto the page.

04:39.000 --> 04:40.320
And we basically created the app.

04:40.320 --> 04:43.360
So if we refresh it, we'll notice nothing's really happening.

04:43.360 --> 04:44.720
And that's totally fine because we haven't

04:44.720 --> 04:47.040
had view display anything yet.

04:47.040 --> 04:49.160
And so when it comes to frameworks,

04:49.160 --> 04:51.400
one of the things that people love about working with frameworks

04:51.400 --> 04:54.560
is that it really helps to track your data across different states.

04:54.560 --> 04:56.200
So that's called reactive data.

04:56.200 --> 04:57.720
So the fact that when something changes,

04:57.720 --> 05:00.360
it'll go ahead and make sure that your HTML page is

05:00.360 --> 05:02.320
updated as expected.

05:02.320 --> 05:05.240
So the way we're going to do this is, inside of our create app,

05:05.240 --> 05:06.960
we're going to pass it an object.

05:06.960 --> 05:08.800
And this object's going to allow us to configure things

05:08.800 --> 05:09.320
about our app.

05:09.320 --> 05:11.360
So think of it as a configuration object.

05:11.360 --> 05:12.320
And what we're going to do is we're

05:12.320 --> 05:15.120
going to pass it a data option.

05:15.120 --> 05:16.400
And what we're going to do is this

05:16.400 --> 05:20.600
is going to be a function that returns an object.

05:20.600 --> 05:22.440
And so we think about what's going on here.

05:22.440 --> 05:24.080
Basically, we're saying, hey, view,

05:24.080 --> 05:25.600
we want you to create an app that's

05:25.600 --> 05:27.320
going to have a data property.

05:27.320 --> 05:29.040
And the reason we're using a function here

05:29.040 --> 05:31.480
is because we want to actually basically maintain

05:31.480 --> 05:33.600
the reactivity in terms of tracking

05:33.600 --> 05:36.160
the latest instance of that object.

05:36.160 --> 05:37.840
Because if you were to do something instead,

05:37.840 --> 05:42.080
like just data like this, the data becomes stale over time.

05:42.080 --> 05:44.120
And there becomes like caching issues.

05:44.120 --> 05:47.280
And so it's being kind of best practice across frameworks

05:47.280 --> 05:49.400
that when you're managing data, you actually

05:49.400 --> 05:51.720
have it be a function that returns an object.

05:51.720 --> 05:54.840
And then the framework will take care of it from there for you.

05:54.840 --> 05:57.000
And so in this, let's go ahead and just add a message here.

05:57.000 --> 06:01.360
And this time, I'll just say, hello, it works.

06:01.360 --> 06:02.560
OK.

06:02.560 --> 06:04.760
So now here, we have our reactive data message.

06:04.760 --> 06:07.000
And the question is, how are we going to actually render

06:07.000 --> 06:08.960
that inside of our app?

06:08.960 --> 06:10.920
Well, the way we're going to do that

06:10.920 --> 06:14.240
is we're going to be using the curly brace syntax.

06:14.240 --> 06:16.720
So this is specific to, well, actually, this

06:16.720 --> 06:18.840
has been a fairly common syntax language.

06:18.840 --> 06:20.880
But as far as the three frameworks are concerned,

06:20.880 --> 06:24.880
this one view uses the double curly brace.

06:24.880 --> 06:27.680
And all we type is message.

06:27.680 --> 06:28.960
That's all it is.

06:28.960 --> 06:30.480
And when we refresh it, you'll notice

06:30.480 --> 06:34.480
your data is now appearing inside of your page,

06:34.480 --> 06:35.240
as you would expect.

